thread:
anya: Heads up — hitting a nasty retry/auth loop in the Kotlin sample when running on Android 11 emulator. Background syncs start, a few 401s, and then we see many refresh calls.
 dave: Which flow are you using for refresh? Authenticator or Interceptor?
 anya: Authenticator in the sample (okhttp3.Authenticator). Code is essentially the basic pattern from the docs.
 rachel: Authenticator runs only on 401 responses but will cause okhttp to re-execute the original call. If refresh is slow you can get concurrent refreshes.
 anya: Example snippet I used:
```
class SimpleAuthenticator : Authenticator {
  override fun authenticate(route: Route?, response: Response): Request? {
    val newToken = tokenManager.refreshBlocking()
    return response.request.newBuilder()
      .header("Authorization", "Bearer $newToken")
      .build()
  }
}
```
 dave: Two common pitfalls: 1) refreshBlocking triggers multiple inflight refreshes, 2) the retried request may still be seen as unauthorized by the server if token propagation is delayed or if there are racey caches.
 anya: Repro steps: start sampleapp with mock server that simulates a 401 on the next request, then returns 200 for subsequent calls. On emulator I saw ~5 refresh calls for one 401.
 rachel: For SDK guidance we should prefer a dedupe pattern: single inflight refresh that other callers suspend on. Also check if request already contains the new token to avoid looping.
 dave: Minimal dedupe sketch (Kotlin coroutines):
```
private val refreshMutex = Mutex()
private var refreshDeferred: CompletableDeferred<String>? = null

suspend fun refreshOnce(): String {
  refreshDeferred?.let { return it.await() }
  refreshMutex.withLock {
    refreshDeferred?.let { return it.await() }
    val deferred = CompletableDeferred<String>()
    refreshDeferred = deferred
    try {
      val t = performRefreshHttpCall()
      deferred.complete(t)
      return t
    } catch (e: Exception) {
      deferred.completeExceptionally(e)
      throw e
    } finally {
      refreshDeferred = null
    }
  }
}
```
 anya: Would that work from Authenticator which is not suspendable? Our sample is currently blocking inside authenticate() and that complicates things.
 rachel: You can bridge by running runBlocking inside authenticate() but be careful — blocking the OkHttp thread pool can starve dispatcher. Better approach: move refresh to an Interceptor where you can suspend (with retrofit-suspend adapter) or use a token refresh service outside okhttp.
 dave: Another practical guard: in authenticate(), check responseCount / priorResponse to avoid infinite retries, and if response.request.header("Authorization") already equals latest known token, return null.
 anya: I added a quick guard:
```
if (responseCount(response) > 1) return null
val current = tokenStore.peek()
if (response.request.header("Authorization") == "Bearer $current") return null
```
 dave: We should document an explicit pattern in devex: "OkHttp refresh — safe patterns for Android". Include: prefer interceptor-based async refresh or dedupe for blocking flows, avoid blocking okhttp dispatcher, check priorResponse, and add small jitter/backoff for concurrent refresh attempts.
 sdk-ci: CI note: created RDW-9411 (labels: devex sdk android) and linked the sample repro. https://redwood.atlassian.net/browse/RDW-9411
 rachel: Also test with okhttp Dispatcher tuned values — emulator with low maxRequestsPerHost can change timing and hide/reveal race conditions.
 anya: I'll open a PR on the sampleapp that replaces the blocking Authenticator with a dedupe-and-guard approach and adds a MockWebServer integration to assert only one refresh occurs. Link coming soon.
 dave: Good. For the SDK guide we should include code for both coroutine-based dedupe and a simple Java-friendly synchronized approach (e.g., AtomicReference<CompletableFuture> or synchronized block) so platform teams can port it.
